// flags: --strict --release
// spec: types.tainted types.secret builtins.escapers decl.function stmt.control auth.policy warnings
// Correct code the checker must accept even under --strict --release: every
// construct here sits next to a rule that rejects something similar.
enum Color { Red, Blue }

struct Config { string name; string api_url; }
struct User { int id; bool admin; }
struct Doc { int owner; }

// Policies may start with any statement, not only `return`.
policy CanEdit(User u, Doc d) {
    if (u.admin) { return true; }
    return u.id == d.owner;
}

// ...including a declaration that starts with the literal true.
policy CanView(User u, Doc d) {
    bool allowed = true;
    if (u.id != d.owner && !u.admin) { allowed = false; }
    return allowed;
}

test "strangers cannot view" { assert_denied CanView(new User{id: 1, admin: false}, new Doc{owner: 2}); }
test "strangers cannot edit" { assert_denied CanEdit(new User{id: 1, admin: false}, new Doc{owner: 2}); }
test "owners can edit" { assert_granted CanEdit(new User{id: 2, admin: false}, new Doc{owner: 2}); }

// char_str() of trusted integers is fine, whether they come from a helper's
// parameter, a function's result or a list of trusted numbers.
string letter(int c) { return char_str(c); }
int code_for(int i) { return 65 + i; }

List<int> trusted_codes() {
    List<int> xs = new List<int>();
    xs.push(33);
    return xs;
}

string describe() { return "issued 3"; }

string greeting() { return "hello"; }
string farewell() { return "bye"; }

// A function may end with exit().
int fail(string why) {
    println("stopping: " + why);
    exit(3);
}

int main() {
    List<int> codes = new List<int>();
    codes.push(72);
    codes.push(105);
    string word = char_str(codes[0]) + letter(codes[1]) + char_str(code_for(1));
    println(word + char_str(trusted_codes()[0]));
    string token_log = describe();
    if (!write_file("/dev/null", token_log)) { return fail("cannot write"); }
    if (greeting() == farewell()) { println("unexpected"); }
    Config cfg = new Config{name: "reports", api_url: "https://example.org/api"};
    println(cfg.name + " " + cfg.api_url);
    Html page = escape_html("<b>literal</b>") + "<br>";
    page += "<hr>";
    page += escape_html("&");
    LogText note = escape_log("a");
    note += escape_log("b");
    println(note);
    if (!write_file("/dev/null", page)) { return fail("cannot write"); }
    Color favourite = Color.Blue;
    if (favourite == Color.Blue) { println("blue"); }
    println(int_to_str(local_named_like_enum()));
    if (let pct = validate_range(taint("42"), 0, 100)) { println(int_to_str(pct)); }
    if (let key = secret_env("CAPSTAN_TEST_NO_SUCH_VARIABLE", 65536)) {
        if (secret_eq(key, "expected")) { println("unexpected secret"); }
    } else {
        println("no secret set");
    }
    return 0;
}

// A local may share an enum's name; inside its scope the name means the local.
int local_named_like_enum() {
    string Color = "shade";
    return Color.len();
}
